Q: Scanner vs. BufferedReader A: <h2>tl;dr</h2>
<p>Use NIO.2 features in modern Java. Simpler code, benefits from buffered reading under-the-covers.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>Files.readAllLines( filePath )  // Read entire file into RAM.
</code></pre>
<h2>NIO.2</h2>
<blockquote>
<p>Scanner vs. BufferedReader</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Neither.</p>
<ul>
<li><code>Scanner</code> is used to “parse primitive types and strings using regular expressions” (<a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/docs/api/java.base/java/util/Scanner.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Javadoc</a>).</li>
<li><a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/docs/api/java.base/java/io/BufferedReader.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>BufferedReader</code></a> is for “buffering characters so as to provide for the efficient reading of characters, arrays, and lines” from a character-input stream. Alternative classes are easier to use while also providing the performance of buffered reading.</li>
</ul>
<p>In modern Java, I suggest using the more convenient features of Java NIO.2.  See <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/javase/8/docs/technotes/guides/io/index.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Java documentation</a> and <a href="https://www.ecosia.org/search?q=java%20nio.2" rel="nofollow noreferrer">other sources of info</a>.</p>
<h3>Small files</h3>
<p>For small files, read the entire content into memory as a <code>List</code> of <code>String</code> objects. We can use the NIO.2 feature <code>Files.readAllLines</code> to read the file while separating each line as a <code>String</code> object.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>List &lt; String &gt; lines = Files.readAllLines( filePath ) ;
</code></pre>
<p>Under the covers, <code>Files.readAllLines</code> <a href="https://github.com/openjdk/jdk/blob/66a34be54a43c110f8dd577775fc213ed912faa7/src/java.base/share/classes/java/nio/file/Files.java#L3088" rel="nofollow noreferrer">uses a buffered reader</a> in its <em>OpenJDK</em> implementation:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>try (BufferedReader reader = newBufferedReader(path, cs)) { …
</code></pre>
<p>To locate a file, use <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/docs/api/java.base/java/nio/file/Paths.html#get(java.lang.String,java.lang.String...)" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Paths.get</code></a>, another NIO.2 feature.</p>
<p>In this full example, notice how we can do the parsing directly, without need of <code>Scanner</code>. We call <code>UUID.fromString</code> to parse <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Universally_unique_identifier" rel="nofollow noreferrer">UUID</a> values in canonical hex format. And we call <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/docs/api/java.base/java/time/LocalDate.html#parse(java.lang.CharSequence)" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>LocalDate.parse</code></a> to parse standard <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISO_8601" rel="nofollow noreferrer">ISO 8601</a> date strings.</p>
<p>When creating a custom class, in Java 16+, make it a <a href="https://openjdk.org/jeps/395" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>record</code></a> if its main purpose is to transparently communicate shallowly-immutable data. A <code>record</code> can be declared locally, as we do here.</p>
<p>In Java 25+, <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/docs/api/java.base/java/lang/IO.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>IO</code></a> class makes a convenient alternative to <code>System.out</code>.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>record Employee(
        UUID id ,
        String name ,
        LocalDate hireDate
) { }
List &lt; Employee &gt; employees = new ArrayList &lt;&gt;();

Path filePath = Paths.get( &quot;/Users/basil_dot_work/example.txt&quot; );
try
{
    Files.readAllLines( filePath , StandardCharsets.UTF_8 );

    IO.println( &quot;Count lines: &quot; + lines.size() );
    for ( String line : lines )
    {
        String[] parts = line.split( &quot;\t&quot; );  // Split by TAB character delimiter.
        UUID id = UUID.fromString( parts[ 0 ] );
        String name = parts[ 1 ];
        LocalDate hireDate = LocalDate.parse( parts[ 2 ] );
        Employee employee = new Employee( id , name , hireDate ); // Assemble into an Employee object.
        employees.add( employee );  // Collect employee objects into a list.
    }

}
catch ( IOException e )
{
    System.err.println( &quot;Error reading file: &quot; + e.getMessage() );
}

employees.forEach( IO :: println );
</code></pre>
<p>When run:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>Count lines: 3
Employee[id=019d3b70-9284-70ab-a42d-ded53416caf6, name=Alice, hireDate=2022-01-23]
Employee[id=019d3b72-cdbc-742e-b583-a9f92077a97b, name=Bob, hireDate=2018-02-23]
Employee[id=019d3b73-1706-7b37-a4ea-d217b66fdfaf, name=Carol, hireDate=2023-03-23]
</code></pre>
<h3>Large files</h3>
<p>You may not want to allocate large amounts of RAM to load a very big file all at once. In that case, read the files in chunks.</p>
<p>We use <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/docs/api/java.base/java/nio/file/Files.html#newBufferedReader(java.nio.file.Path,java.nio.charset.Charset)" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Files.newBufferedReader</code></a> to conveniently create a <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/docs/api/java.base/java/io/BufferedReader.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>BufferedReader</code></a>. This <code>BufferedReader</code> object needs to be closed when no longer needed. That class implements <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/docs/api/java.base/java/lang/AutoCloseable.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>AutoCloseable</code></a>, so we can use <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/javase/tutorial/essential/exceptions/tryResourceClose.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">try-with-resources</a> syntax to automatically close it.</p>
<p>In contrast to code above, here we use a <code>static</code> factory method to instantiate an <code>Employee</code> object from each line of text read from file. This makes our code easier to read. To enable that separate method, we move our <code>record</code> definition from being local to being nested.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>public class ReadLargeFile
{
    static void main ( )
    {
        IO.println( Runtime.version() );

        List &lt; Employee &gt; employees = new ArrayList &lt;&gt;();

        Path filePath = Paths.get( &quot;/Users/basil_dot_work/example.txt&quot; );
        try
                (
                        BufferedReader reader = Files.newBufferedReader( filePath , StandardCharsets.UTF_8 )
                )
        {
            String line;
            int lineNumber = 1;

            while ( null != ( line = reader.readLine() ) )
            {
                final Employee employee = parseEmployeeLine( line );
                employees.add( employee );  // Collect employee objects into a list.
            }

        }
        catch ( IOException e )
        {
            System.err.println( &quot;Error reading file: &quot; + e.getMessage() );
        }

        employees.forEach( IO :: println );
    }

    private static Employee parseEmployeeLine ( final String line )
    {
        Objects.requireNonNull( line );
        String[] parts = line.split( &quot;\t&quot; );  // Split by TAB character delimiter.
        UUID id = UUID.fromString( parts[ 0 ] );
        String name = parts[ 1 ];
        LocalDate hireDate = LocalDate.parse( parts[ 2 ] );
        return new Employee( id , name , hireDate );
    }

    record Employee(
            UUID id ,
            String name ,
            LocalDate hireDate
    ) { }
}
</code></pre>
<p>When run:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>26+35
Employee[id=019d3b70-9284-70ab-a42d-ded53416caf6, name=Alice, hireDate=2022-01-23]
Employee[id=019d3b72-cdbc-742e-b583-a9f92077a97b, name=Bob, hireDate=2018-02-23]
Employee[id=019d3b73-1706-7b37-a4ea-d217b66fdfaf, name=Carol, hireDate=2023-03-23]
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>In real work, I would add some input validation. For example, using try-catch to trap for <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/docs/api/java.base/java/time/format/DateTimeParseException.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>DateTimeParseException</code></a> in case of faulty date input. And I would use <a href="https://jspecify.dev/" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><em>JSpecify</em></a> annotations to enable null-checking within my <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Integrated_development_environment" rel="nofollow noreferrer">IDE</a>.</p>
